        "explanation": "Bronchoscopy is indicated for investigation of haemoptysis, particularly with risk factors such as age >40, smoking history, and abnormal chest imaging suggestive of malignancy [Source: eTG Complete 2024 - Haemoptysis Investigation]. This patient's hilar mass on CXR raises strong suspicion for lung cancer, making bronchoscopy essential for tissue diagnosis and visualisation of endobronchial lesions [Source: TSANZ Guidelines 2023 - Bronchoscopy Indications]. Flexible bronchoscopy allows direct visualisation of airways, endobronchial biopsy, brushings, and lavage for cytology [Source: UpToDate 2024 - Flexible Bronchoscopy]. For central/hilar masses, bronchoscopy has diagnostic yield of 70-90% [Source: eTG Complete 2024 - Lung Cancer Diagnosis]. Other key indications for bronchoscopy include: persistent infiltrate, suspected endobronchial obstruction, staging of known lung cancer (combined with EBUS), assessment of airway prior to surgery, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) for suspected infection or interstitial lung disease, and foreign body removal [Source: TSANZ Guidelines 2023 - Bronchoscopy]. Option A is incorrect; haemoptysis is an indication, not contraindication, though massive haemoptysis (>200-300 mL/24h) requires stabilisation first. Option C is incorrect; bronchoscopy is preferred for central lesions with potential endobronchial component. Option D is incorrect; diagnostic bronchoscopy should not be delayed. Option E is not indicated in absence of clinical infection. Contraindications to bronchoscopy include: severe hypoxaemia refractory to supplemental oxygen, unstable cardiovascular status, severe coagulopathy, and recent myocardial infarction [Source: UpToDate 2024 - Bronchoscopy Contraindications].",
